      "writtenOn": "2023-12-13T16:30:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "One of the big strengths of Rust is how strongly typed it is; could we keep\n`Vsid` and `Sid` as distinct types (even if both are actually `u32`) to prevent us from inadvertently swapping them? If this makes the code too complicated, we can drop it but I\u0027m thinking that `parse_iommus\u003cT,S\u003e where S: From\u003cu32\u003e` might make it work?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 236,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 236,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "f8839be73792bd44a94f92be3142649db8ec7a6d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7410bbe3_d60f48fe",
        "filename": "pvmfw/src/device_assignment.rs",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 236,
      "author": {
        "id": 1019013
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-12-14T08:23:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is `Vsid` and `Sid` different? I thought that `Sid` can be applied to both `vSID` and `pSID`. Correct me if I\u0027m wrong.",
      "parentUuid": "6100c551_b47c2db2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 236,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 236,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "f8839be73792bd44a94f92be3142649db8ec7a6d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3126fed4_73f4f6ed",
        "filename": "pvmfw/src/device_assignment.rs",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 236,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882526
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-12-14T11:32:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, vSIDs have a single format (`u32`) while SIDs are hardware-defined and can be quite different from a single `u32`. Also, they\u0027re in different \"namespaces\" so things like comparing them don\u0027t make sense; hence my suggestion of keeping distinct types.\n\nI guess, from the perspective of pvmfw, we\u0027re not really manipulating the hardware SIDs but some tokenized version, in which case, we\u0027re limited (with the current API) to the single CPU register returned by the HVC i.e. the \"token\" SID is a `u64`. pvmfw does not need to (and shouldn\u0027t) be aware of the actual tokenization algorithm, only than it produces a physIOMMU-unique token per mapping.\n\nWe\u0027re currently storing that value as a single DT cell (i.e. `u32`) in `/host` but at some point, might need to double that (`iommu-cells\u003d2`?) to match the HVC. When/if that happens, vSIDs will remain `u32`, which is only possible with distinct types.",
      "parentUuid": "7410bbe3_d60f48fe",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 236,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 236,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "f8839be73792bd44a94f92be3142649db8ec7a6d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
